---
// In-page contents: the article's own h2s as anchors.
// specs/pages/writing-article.md §2.2.
//
// A table of contents over two headings is noise, so the callers only render
// this once an article has three — the filtering and the threshold are
// theirs, and by the time this component mounts the condition is already
// spent. That is why the grid placement below is unconditional: an article
// with no contents list has no Contents element to place.
//
// Rendered in the sticky column on wide viewports and inline above the body
// below the breakpoint — one component, one source position, no disclosure
// widget. The column binds to the inline start, so it lands on the right
// under dir="rtl".
//
// Sticky IN-PAGE navigation, which is not site navigation — and that
// distinction is why the global header is not sticky and this is
// (specs/navigation.md §1). It pins 24px below the viewport edge, is capped
// at the viewport minus that gap top and bottom, and scrolls inside itself
// when an article has more headings than fit. `min-[900px]:self-start` is how
// a grid item keeps its own height instead of stretching to fill the row: it
// compiles to `align-self: flex-start`, and outside a flex formatting context
// that keyword behaves as `start`, which is what the stylesheet wrote. Named
// in its prefixed form because the scanner reads this comment and the
// unprefixed spelling would emit a rule nothing uses (verdict §4.4).
//
// No scroll-spy: an active-section indicator needs a scroll listener, which
// is JavaScript on the reading path (§2.2). The list shows the article's
// shape, not the reader's position in it.
//
// ── why the class names were prefixed, and why it still matters ───────────
//
// The prefix on the old class names was not decoration. Tailwind generates a
// utility for every bare token it finds in a class attribute, so a component
// class named after a utility word is silently shadowed by the generated
// rule — and one of those words sets `display: contents`, which takes an
// element out of the box tree entirely. Its children get auto-placed, and the
// sticky column comes apart. That is what shipped until the RTL stage's
// screenshot showed the heading list stranded at the foot of the page.
//
// The classes are gone now and the hazard is not: the reason the generated
// rule won was that it was generated at all, so the same collision is one
// unprefixed component class away on any element on the site.
//
// ── the Hebrew heading ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Hebrew is unicameral: no text-transform, and letter-spacing at the keyword
// `normal` rather than at a computed zero (specs/design/typography.md §6,
// specs/navigation.md N-7). Written as two exclusive branches rather than as
// a base with a Hebrew override — utilities land in one layer where Tailwind's
// sort decides ties, so an element carrying both `uppercase` and
// `normal-case` would resolve by a rule this file does not control.
//
// ── the items ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The edge and the 12px beside it are `border-s-2` and `ps-3`, bound to the
// inline axis and never to the left. This component renders on the Hebrew
// article route under dir="rtl", where both belong on the right; a physical
// utility would put the edge on the far side of every heading and the LTR
// screenshots would go on passing. `py-1.5` is padding-block, which does not
// flip and is not meant to.
//
// `[&:hover]:` and never `hover:`: Tailwind v4 wraps `hover:` in
// `@media (hover: hover)`, which would delete the hover state on every touch
// device.

import type { MarkdownHeading } from 'astro';

interface Props {
  /** Already filtered to depth 2 and known to be >= 3 by the caller */
  headings: MarkdownHeading[];
  lang: 'en' | 'he';
}

const { headings, lang } = Astro.props;

const title = lang === 'he' ? 'תוכן העניינים' : 'contents';
---

<nav
  class="mbe-10 min-[900px]:col-start-1 min-[900px]:row-start-2 min-[900px]:sticky min-[900px]:inset-bs-6 min-[900px]:self-start min-[900px]:max-block-[calc(100vh-48px)] min-[900px]:overflow-y-auto"
  aria-labelledby="contents-title"
>
  <p
    class:list={[
      'font-mono text-size-label text-muted pbe-3 border-be border-be-border',
      lang === 'he' ? 'normal-case tracking-[normal]' : 'uppercase tracking-label',
    ]}
    id="contents-title"
  >
    {title}
  </p>
  <ol class="list-none m-0 p-0 mbs-3">
    {
      headings.map((heading) => (
        <li>
          <a
            class="block font-mono text-size-meta leading-tight text-muted no-underline py-1.5 ps-3 border-s-2 border-s-border transition-[color] duration-(--motion-hover) ease-[ease] motion-reduce:transition-none [&:hover]:text-text [&:hover]:border-s-border-strong"
            href={`#${heading.slug}`}
          >{heading.text}</a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>
</nav>
